course: dictionary
lesson: 3293
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Look at the words around the tricky word, because they are its context. «The Church of England is the established church of England and part of the worldwide Anglican Communion.» Here established means official: it is the church the country accepts as its own.
explain: Establish has four main meanings: to set something up so it lasts, to prove a fact, to become well known or accepted, and to make a contact or relationship. Ask yourself what is being established. A rule, a school or a business is set up. A fact is proved. A person becomes accepted. A relationship is made.
explain: Then test your meaning. Put it in the sentence in place of the hard word. If «set up» or «prove for sure» fits, you have found the right meaning.
fact: To establish something means to start or set it up so that it lasts.
fact: To establish a fact means to find it out or prove it for sure.
fact: «Established» can mean official, as in «the established church».
fact: On a shop sign, «established» often shows the year the shop first opened.
fact: You can check any new meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The Church of England is the established church of England.» What does established mean here? | *The official church of the country | A church that is falling down | A church with a tall bell tower | A new church built last year
exercise: Scientists must establish whether the medicine works. What does establish mean here? | *To find out and prove for sure | To paint or decorate | To forget a plan | To lose something important
exercise: «The Romans established many towns across Britain.» Find and copy the word that means started so that they would last. | *established | Romans | towns | across
test: «She has established herself as the best singer in the choir.» What does established mean here? | Lost her voice | Left the choir | *Became well known and accepted | Sat down quietly
test: Why might a shop sign say «established 1952»? | *To show the year it first opened | To warn you of a sale | To give the owner's name | To tell you the price of the goods
exam: Which meaning fits «The school council wants to establish a new rule about lunch»? | To read a rule aloud | To forget a rule | To break a rule | *To set up a rule so that it lasts
